    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Aylett sits where Virginia State Route 360 crosses the Mattaponi River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aylett%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors William Aylett, whose family ran prominent warehouses and mills in the 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aylett%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1781, Todd’s Bridge crossed the Mattaponi and joined the Washington–Rochambeau Revolutionary Route.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4,
        11,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aylett%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Aylett once functioned as a port town on the stage route linking Richmond to Tappahannock, overlooking the river.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/aylett_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Before European arrival, the Pamunkey and Mattaponi tribes inhabited the area that is now Aylett.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/aylett_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Fertile alluvial soils along the Mattaponi valleys have long supported tobacco and soybean farming.",
